\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Per-layer parameter std by component type across the Qwen-2.5 family. Attention and MLP weights are nearly flat across layers (std $\sim $0.01--0.03), while norm layers vary by over an order of magnitude and show pronounced structure---particularly in early and late layers. The dotted line marks $\sigma =0.005$. At every layer and every scale, norm stds sit 1--2 orders of magnitude above weight matrix stds, reinforcing the observation that a fixed absolute $\sigma $ perturbs these component families very differently.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:weight_layers}{{5}{8}{Per-layer parameter std by component type across the Qwen-2.5 family. Attention and MLP weights are nearly flat across layers (std $\sim $0.01--0.03), while norm layers vary by over an order of magnitude and show pronounced structure---particularly in early and late layers. The dotted line marks $\sigma =0.005$. At every layer and every scale, norm stds sit 1--2 orders of magnitude above weight matrix stds, reinforcing the observation that a fixed absolute $\sigma $ perturbs these component families very differently.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Best ensemble accuracy (left) and mean reward delta (right) under absolute vs.\ relative-norm perturbations, each at its respective optimal $\sigma $. The two perturbation families produce very similar results at each scale.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:phase3}{{6}{8}{Best ensemble accuracy (left) and mean reward delta (right) under absolute vs.\ relative-norm perturbations, each at its respective optimal $\sigma $. The two perturbation families produce very similar results at each scale.\relax }{figure.caption.14}{}}
